            <ArticleTitle>Psychosocial effects of enhanced external counterpulsation in the angina patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>494-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Enhanced external counterpulsation (EECP) is a noninvasive pantaloon device designed to increase coronary artery flow in the treatment of angina. This pilot study, conducted in 1992-1993, which used psychosocial testing pre- and posttreatment, yielded data suggesting that EECP is well tolerated psychosocially and produces improvement in the anginal syndrome. More comprehensive research is under way to test these preliminary conclusions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
